1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,483
Day 52: Weight-Space Programming has a language model write

2
00:00:04,483 --> 00:00:08,509
another model's weights by hand, no gradient descent,

3
00:00:08,509 --> 00:00:09,497
MIT licensed.

4
00:00:09,947 --> 00:00:14,019
Three frontier LLMs get only ten ARC science questions and

5
00:00:14,019 --> 00:00:16,265
write a model's weights by hand.

6
00:00:16,265 --> 00:00:20,547
Free to design, they all converge on a sparse fact table read

7
00:00:20,547 --> 00:00:22,021
by a fixed procedure.

8
00:00:22,471 --> 00:00:26,403
I took the writer's role myself, reading the same task,

9
00:00:26,403 --> 00:00:29,691
and wrote 155 short science facts from memory,

10
00:00:29,691 --> 00:00:32,050
nothing from the hidden test set.

11
00:00:32,500 --> 00:00:36,791
Build.py turns each fact into an idf-weighted word vector.

12
00:00:36,791 --> 00:00:40,490
Arch.py, a fixed tensor program with no branching,

13
00:00:40,490 --> 00:00:45,004
compares each option against every fact by cosine similarity.

14
00:00:45,454 --> 00:00:50,112
The repository's own scorer ran this on the real ARC test set,

15
00:00:50,112 --> 00:00:51,991
3,530 held-out questions,

16
00:00:51,991 --> 00:00:54,997
the exact code the paper's authors used.

17
00:00:55,447 --> 00:00:59,353
A single hand-written pass scored 31.3 percent on the test

18
00:00:59,353 --> 00:01:01,305
set, six points above chance,

19
00:01:01,305 --> 00:01:05,009
from a weight table where only 2 percent of entries are

20
00:01:05,009 --> 00:01:05,548
nonzero.

21
00:01:05,998 --> 00:01:09,047
Full write-up in Japanese on Zenn.

22
00:01:09,047 --> 00:01:13,532
Code is MIT licensed, the ARC dataset is CC BY-SA.
